1.5.9.m:
For thus the gods did;
thus the gods did;
and thus indeed men do.
The gods said:
'If we perform this sacrifice aloud,
then the asuras, evil, will find us.
Let us perform the upasads silently;
thus the asuras, evil, will not find us.'
They performed the upasad silently,
having recited only three kindling verses (sāmidhenīs).

having offered three successive oblations,
offers the upasad with the dipping spoon:
'Fierce speech I have struck down, violent speech I have struck down, svāhā.'
Hunger and thirst are indeed fierce speech;
sin and the slaying of an enemy are violent speech.
Verily, that fourfold evil the sacrificer strikes away.
Having brought [the day] to a close, they seized the victim;
thereby the gods struck away destitution, evil, [and] death from the day.
As if having brought [it] to a close, they proceeded from the night;
thereby the gods struck away destitution, evil, [and] death from the night.

1.5.10.m:
They praised towards him with the seventeenfold [stoma];
they took him with the seventeenfold;
they bore him with the seventeenfold,
as great as is the measure of the seventeenfold.
While he was being borne by the seventeenfold, his fiery energy, his brilliant glow fell away.
They praised towards him with the twenty-onefold [stoma];
they took him with the twenty-onefold;
they bore him with the twenty-onefold,
as great as is the measure of the twenty-onefold.
When they chant praise with the ninefold,

1.6.1.m:
He offers in a natural barren spot or in a cleft;
for this is the abode of Nirṛti.
In her own abode he appeases Nirṛti by offering a portion.
'This is your share, o Nirṛti,' he says;
he assigns [it] to her.
'You are prosperous, rich in oblations,' he says;
he turns towards prosperity.
'Release this one from distress,' he says;
he releases him from distress.
He offers with the two thumbs.

1.6.2.m:
'Sacrifice to me first;
with me as the mouth you shall conquer the asuras.'
'To me second,' Soma said;
'with me as king you shall conquer.'
'To me third,' Savitṛ [said];
'impelled by me you shall conquer.'
'To me fourth,' Sarasvatī [said];
'I shall place physical power in you.'
'To me fifth,' Pūṣan [said];
'with me as firm foundation you shall conquer.'

With Agni as the mouth they conquered the asuras,
with Soma as king,
impelled by Savitṛ;
Sarasvatī placed physical power [in them];
Pūṣan was the firm foundation.
Then indeed the gods were victorious.
In that these oblations are poured out, it is for victory.
He does not heap up the high altar;
for the high altar is domestic animals,
and domestic animals are, as it were, unborn at that time.

1.7.3.m:
If he were to offer on the same day,
he would secure only as much wish as one secures with a single oblation.
He offers day after day;
he secures indeed a greater wish;
he attains more sacrificial rites.
He offers a caru to Bṛhaspati at the home of the Brahmin priest (brahman);
right from the mouth he sharpens regal power with brahman for him;
moreover, he causes regal power to grasp hold of brahman.
A white-backed [ox] is the sacrificial fee, for success.

An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] to Indra at the home of the Rājanya;
he secures Indra-power itself.
A bull is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru to Aditi at the home of the chief queen (mahiṣī);
Aditi is indeed this [earth];
he establishes himself on this [earth].
A milch cow is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru to Bhaga at the home of the favorite wife (vāvātā);
he places fortune into him.
A six-month-pregnant four-year-old cow is the sacrificial fee, for success.

A caru to Nirṛti at the home of the discarded wife (parivṛktī), [made] of black rice grain split with fingernails;
he appeases Nirṛti, evil itself.
A black, hornless cow is the sacrificial fee, for success.
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni at the home of the commander of the army (senānī);
he sharpens his army.
Gold is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A ten-pot-sherd [cake] to Varuṇa at the home of the charioteer/herald (sūta);
he secures the consecration of Varuṇa.
A castrated bull is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A seven-pot-sherd [cake] to the Maruts at the home of the village headman (grāmaṇī);

the Maruts are food;
he secures food.
A dappled [cow] is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to Savitṛ at the home of the chamberlain (kṣattṛ), for impulse;
a grey-speckled [animal] is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A two-pot-sherd [cake] to the Aśvins at the home of the charioteer (saṃgrahītṛ);
the two Aśvins are indeed the physicians of the gods;
through them he prepares medicine for him.
Two twin calves are the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru to Pūṣan at the home of the carver/collector (bhāgadugha);

Pūṣan is food;
he secures food.
A black [animal] is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru made of gavidhukā grass to Rudra at the home of the superintendent of dice (akṣāvāpa);
at the end he appeases Rudra.
A speckled ox with raised horns is the sacrificial fee, for success.
These are twelve oblations.
The year has twelve months;
through the year he secures the realm for him.
He indeed comes to have a realm.

He covers the offering to Bṛhaspati with the offering to Mitra;
he places brahman and regal power together in harmony for him;
moreover, he establishes regal power firmly in brahman.
He proceeds first with the offering to Bṛhaspati;
right from the mouth he sharpens brahman for him;
moreover, he causes regal power to grasp hold of brahman.
The altar is self-made;
the sacrificial grass is self-cut;
the kindling wood is self-made;
for the conquest of what is unconquered.
Therefore the forest is conquered for kings.
That same white cow with a white calf is the sacrificial fee, for success.

1.7.4.m:
These are the oblations of the divine quickeners (devasūs).
There are so many consecrations of the gods;
they bestow consecrations upon him;
they consecrate him.
Agni consecrates him [as lord] of householders;
Soma of lord of the trees;
Rudra of domestic animals;
Bṛhaspati of speech;
Indra of the elders;
Mitra of truths;

Varuṇa of the lords of sacred law (dharma).
This becomes everything.
'May Savitṛ quicken you among the impulses,' [saying this] he takes [his] hand for impulse.
'You gods who are the divine quickeners,' he says;
this is strictly according to the yajus.
'For great regal power, for great overlordship, for great realm of the people,' he says;
he expresses this very wish.
'This is your king, O Bharatas; Soma is the king of us Brahmins,' he says;
therefore Brahmins have Soma as king.
'This royal power has been established in its name,' he says;

he firmly places royal power in him.
'Varuṇa has laid his own body upon [him],' he says;
he secures the consecration of Varuṇa.
'We have become devotees of pure Mitra,' he says;
he makes him pure and observant of vows.
'We have thought on the name of the great cosmic order (ṛta),' he says;
he honors him.
'All the troops have become Varuṇa's,' he says;
he makes him one whose troops are all.
'Mitra has overcome enmity with his courses,' he says;

he makes him overcome enmity.
'The sacrificial ones have gladdened [him] by the cosmic order,' he says;
he sweetens him.
'Trita has thoroughly reached our old age,' he says;
he places lifespan into him.
He wipes [his hands] with two [formulas];
two-footed is the sacrificer, for firm standing.
He cuts off portions together for Agni Sviṣṭakṛt from the eleven-pot-sherd [cake] for Agni-Soma and from the oblations of the divine quickeners;
he encloses him on both sides with deities.
He strides the Viṣṇu-strides;
becoming Viṣṇu himself, he conquers these worlds.

1.7.5.m:
'You have arrived for the purpose,' [saying this] he offers an oblation;
by means of the oblation he redeems them and takes [them].
Moreover, he draws [water] from those [waters] that are made into sacrificial matter and sprinkled with clarified butter.
He draws [waters] that are flowing;
these indeed are the realm of the waters;
he draws the realm for him.
Moreover, they bear him to prosperity.
'You are the lord of the waters,' he says;
he has produced a pair.
'You are a virile wave,' he says;

1.7.8.m:
He should offer in her house, reciting: 'O Prajāpati, other than you none [has encompassed all these creations],'—
[in the house of her] of whom he desires that her offspring should be the realm.
Her offspring becomes the realm.
The Adhvaryu anoints with [a cup] made of parṇa-wood;
he places spiritual preeminence and brilliance in him.
A Rājanya [anoints] with [a cup] of udumbara-wood;
he places vigor and food in him.
A Vaiśya [anoints] with [a cup] of aśvattha-wood;
he places the people and nourishment in him.
A clansman [anoints] with [a cup] of nyagrodha-wood;
he prepares friends for him,
and also for firm standing.

1.7.9.m:
Six are the seasons;
with the seasons he yokes it.
He takes the strides of Viṣṇu;
becoming Viṣṇu he conquers these worlds.
The kṣatriya who is set in place
takes hold of him from behind;
he becomes the realm.
He takes hold from behind with a triṣṭubh;
the triṣṭubh is sensory power;
he places sensory power in the sacrificer.

'Homage to mother earth,' he says, for non-injury.
'Thou art so much, thou art lifespan, bestow lifespan on me,' he says;
he places vigor into himself.
'Thou art vigor, bestow vigor on me,' he says;
he places vigor into himself.
'Thou art yoked, thou art luster, bestow luster upon me,' he says;
he places luster into himself.
He brings [these offerings] together to the brahman priest in a single act;
in a single act the sacrificer thus bestows lifespan, vigor, and luster [upon himself].
He offers the chariot-unloosing oblations for firm standing.

There are three horses;
the chariot is the fourth.
Therefore he makes four offerings.
If both were to step down together,
they would go to the same world.
Together with the charioteer he places the chariot on the chariot-stand;
he thereby screens him from the heavenly realm.
'The swan seated in the clear [sky],' with this [verse] he places it;
he brings it down with the brahman;
he places it with the brahman.
He places it with an aticchandas verse;
verily, the aticchandas is all the meters;
he places it with all the meters.
This is the summit of the meters,
namely the aticchandas;
because he places it with an aticchandas,
he makes him the summit among his equals.

1.7.10.m:
'Thou art Mitra, thou art Varuṇa,' he says.
Day belongs to Mitra;
night belongs to Varuṇa.
By both day and night he brings him down.
'Thou art Mitra, thou art Varuṇa,' he says.
The right [side] belongs to Mitra;
the left belongs to Varuṇa.
The clotted curds belong to the All-Gods;
he brings them down to their own portion.
'Together with all the gods I [stand],' he says.

Offspring belong to the All-Gods;
he makes them eaters.
'Thou art the navel of regal power, thou art the womb of regal power,' saying this, he spreads the upper cover for unity of origin.
'Sit upon the pleasant, sit upon the well-seated [seat],' he says;
this is strictly according to the sacrificial formula.
'May it not harm thee, may it not harm me,' he says, for non-injury.
'Varuṇa, of firm vows, has sat down among his dwellings for universal rule, he of good purpose,' he says;
he makes him one of universal rule, of good purpose.
'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Savitṛ of true impulse,' he says;
he makes him Savitṛ of true impulse.

'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Indra of true splendorous might,' he says;
he makes him Indra of true splendorous might.
'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Mitra the most benevolent,' he says;
he makes him Mitra the most benevolent.
'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Varuṇa of true ordinances,' he says;
he makes him Varuṇa of true ordinances.
'You are Savitṛ of true impulse,' he says;
with this he proclaims the gāyatrī.
'You are Indra of true splendorous might,' he says;
with this he proclaims the triṣṭubh.

'You are Mitra the most benevolent,' he says;
with this he proclaims the jagatī.
These deities are truth;
these meters are truth;
he secures truth.
'You are Varuṇa of true ordinances,' he says;
with this he proclaims the anuṣṭubh.
The anuṣṭubh is truth and untruth;
Varuṇa is truth and untruth;
he secures truth and untruth.

Truth and untruth, when spoken, do not injure him
who knows thus.
'Thou art Indra's bolt, slayer of Vṛtra,' saying this, he hands him the wooden sword;
for the wooden sword is the thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he subdues for him his rivals lower and further.
For so it is better,
that these should be subdued for him.
'This king has overcome the directions,' saying this, he hands him five dice;
these are indeed all the winning throws;
he makes him one who never loses at play.

They proclaim the boiled rice mess [for the king];
he is the highest deity,
namely the boiled rice mess;
he causes him to attain the highest distinction.
'Possessing glorious renown, auspicious, of true kingship!' he says;
this is the blessing he invokes.
He causes the story of Śunaḥśepa to be recited;
he frees him from Varuṇa's noose.
More than a hundred [cows] are given;
man has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundred faculties;
he firmly stands in lifespan, in Indra's power.
He cuts portions for Agni Sviṣṭakṛt from [the cake] on twenty-one potsherds for the Maruts and from the clotted curds for the All-Gods;
he encompasses him on both sides with deities.
'To the Son of the Waters, svāhā! To the Son of Vigor, svāhā! To Agni, lord of the house, svāhā!' with this he offers three oblations.
These worlds are three;
he firmly stands in these worlds.

1.8.1.m:
When Varuṇa had consecrated himself [by pressing soma], his Indra's power and heroic power flew away in ten portions.
He crept after it with the creeping [offerings];
that is the creeping-nature of the creeping [offerings].
With Agni as the deity he harnessed [it] on the first day;
with Sarasvatī as speech on the second;
with Savitṛ as impulse on the third;
with Pūṣan as beasts on the fourth;
with Bṛhaspati as brahman on the fifth;
with Indra as the deity on the sixth;
with Varuṇa, his own deity, on the seventh;

with King Soma on the eighth;
with Tvaṣṭṛ as form on the ninth;
with Viṣṇu as the sacrifice he attained it [on the tenth].
In that there are creeping [offerings],
the sacrificer thereby attains Indra's power and heroic power.
Each preceding sacrificial altar becomes higher and higher,
for the securing of Indra's power and heroic power.
Before the upasads he proceeds with [the oblation] to Soma;
for Soma is the placer of semen;
he thus deposits the semen.
Between them [he proceeds] with that to Tvaṣṭṛ;
when semen is placed, Tvaṣṭṛ fashions forms.
Above, [he proceeds] with that to Viṣṇu;
for Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he firmly stands in the sacrifice at the end.

1.8.2.m:
for the attainment of Prajāpati.
He gives two mirrors to the Adhvaryu;
he causes him to attain renown.
A garland to the Udgātṛ;
he shines forth for him.
A golden breastplate to the Hotṛ;
he leads up the sun for him.
A horse to the Prastotṛ and Pratihartṛ;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati,
for the attainment of Prajāpati.

Twelve four-year-old cows to the Brahman priest;
he secures lifespan.
A barren cow to the Maitrāvaruṇa priest;
he makes the realm submissive to him.
A bull to the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin priest;
he makes the realm endowed with power.
Two garments to the Neṣṭṛ and Potṛ priests;
these two are his strainers.
A one-ox cart laden with barley to the Acchāvāka priest;
from the very end he appeases Varuṇa.

An ox to the Āgnīdhra priest;
the ox is indeed a bearer,
the Āgnīdhra is a bearer;
by means of the bearer, he secures the bearer of the sacrifice.
When Indra had pressed [soma], his capacity and heroic power flew away in three parts:
Bhṛgu became one-third,
the Śrāyantīya [sāman] one-third,
Sarasvatī one-third.
A descendant of Bhṛgu is the Hotṛ;
the Śrāyantīya is the Brahma-sāman;
the Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman;
he takes the waters of the Sarasvatī,
for the securing of capacity and heroic power.
The Śrāyantīya is the Brahma-sāman;
he confers capacity and heroic power upon him.
The Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman;
he secures capacity and heroic power in him.

He is indeed likely to go mad following the quarters,
whom they cause to stand along the quarters.
There are the Aveṣṭis of the quarters;
he becomes established in the quarters, so as not to go mad.
He sacrifices to five deities;
five are the quarters;
he becomes established in the quarters.
After each offering, he causes the Bārhaspatya [portion] to be sprinkled with clarified butter;
Bṛhaspati has the sacrificer as his deity;
he enriches the sacrificer with fiery energy.

1.8.3.m:
He offers a dewlapped, pregnant [heifer] to Aditi,
a speckled four-year-old heifer to the Maruts.
He puts the common people and the realm in harmony for him.
He proceeds with that for Aditi first,
with that for the Maruts afterwards;
he fastens the common people to the realm.
He calls for the Śraukṣat loudly for the one to Aditi,
in a whisper for the one to the Maruts;
therefore the realm commands the common people.
The [heifer] for Aditi is pregnant;

the embryo is capacity;
he makes the realm endowed with capacity.
The [heifer] for the Maruts is not pregnant;
the Maruts are the common people;
he makes the common people devoid of capacity.
The gods and the asuras were in conflict;
the gods, having deposited the truth of speech with the Aśvins and Pūṣan,
overcame the asuras by means of untruth.
They offered a cake on twelve potsherds to the Aśvins and to Pūṣan;
thence they secured the truth of speech.

When he offers a cake on twelve potsherds to the Aśvins and to Pūṣan,
having overcome his rivals by means of untruth,
he secures the truth of speech.
A mess of boiled grain to Sarasvat, whose speech is true;
[what was] spoken before,
he confirms with what follows.
A cake on twelve potsherds to Savitṛ of true impulse, for impulse.
He dispatches messengers;
these are declarations;
they cause him to attain renown;
moreover, he is not cut off from the messengers.
A bow with three arrows and a dry leather bag are the dakṣiṇā, for prosperity.

He offers [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni;
therefore in the cold season the Kuru-Pañcālas go eastward.
A mess of boiled grain to Soma;
therefore settling down in spring, they feast.
[A cake] on twelve potsherds to Savitṛ;
therefore prior to the barleys they ward [them] off by means of Savitṛ.
A mess of boiled grain to Bṛhaspati;
having warded off by means of Savitṛ,
by means of brahman they set the barleys [to grow].
[A cake] on eight potsherds to Tvaṣṭṛ;

1.8.4.m:
thereby they produce the forms.
[A cake] on twelve potsherds to Vaiśvānara;
therefore in the late summer heat the Kuru-Pañcālas go westward.
He offers a mess of boiled grain to Sarasvatī;
therefore in the rainy season all voices speak.
Through [the offering] to Pūṣan they settle down;
through [the offering] to Mitra they plow;
through [the offering] to Varuṇa they remain restrained;
through [the offering] to Kṣetrapati they ripen [crops];
through [the offering] to Aditi they deposit [grain].

When Indra had pressed [soma], his capacity and heroic power flew away in ten parts.
What he first spat out,
that became the jujube berry;
what [he spat out] the second time,
that became the jujube fruit;
what [he spat out] the third time,
that became the wild jujube berry;
what [came] from his nose,
that was the lion;
what [came] from his two eyes,

1.8.5.m:
that was the tiger;
what [came] from his two ears,
that was the wolf;
what was upward,
that was Soma;
what was downward,
that was the surā liquor.
There are three kinds of groats,
for securing capacity;
[there are] three kinds of hairs.

Prajāpati is unspecified;
[it is] for obtaining Prajāpati.
He draws with a single verse;
he places heroic power in the sacrificer singly.
He seizes a smoke-colored [victim] for the Aśvins;
the Aśvins indeed are the two physicians of the gods;
through the two of them he makes medicine for him.
[He seizes] a ram for Sarasvatī;
Sarasvatī indeed is speech;
by speech indeed he heals him.
[He seizes] a bull for Indra, for possession of Indra's nature.

1.8.6.m:
If he were to seize them at three sacrificial posts,
he would place physical faculty and heroic power outside of him;
he would generate a rival for him.
He seizes them at a single sacrificial post;
he places physical faculty and heroic power in him in one way only;
he does not generate a rival for him.
There are no sacrificial cakes for these domestic animals;
for these are graha-cakes.
With: 'Ye, the surā-drink, O Aśvins', the two offering verses belong to all the gods;
he pleases all the deities.

He should hire a Brahmin as the drinker of the remnant;
for the Brahmin is the drinker of the remnant of the oblation.
If he should not find a Brahmin,
he should pour it down into an anthill;
that indeed is the atonement for it.
Whatever is defective in the Sautrāmaṇī,
that becomes successful for him.
There are domestic animals and sacrificial cakes dedicated to various deities for success.
The one for Indra is the last of the domestic animals;
the one for Indra is the first of the sacrificial cakes.

He places in him physical faculties that correspond to one another.
He proceeds with the sacrificial cakes before the after-offerings;
the sacrificial cakes indeed are domestic animals;
he secures domestic animals.
He pours out [the grain for] an eleven-potsherd [cake] for Indra;
he secures physical faculty.
[He pours out grain for] a twelve-potsherd [cake] for Savitṛ for impulse;
[and grain for] a ten-potsherd [cake] for Varuṇa;
at the end indeed he appeases Varuṇa by sacrifice.
A mare is the sacrificial fee.

For she brings forth both a horse
and a mule;
both soma and surā;
that the Sautrāmaṇī is for success.
For one excessively purified by soma, he seizes a domestic animal for Bṛhaspati as a fourth;
Bṛhaspati indeed is the brahman of the gods;
by the brahman he remedies what is defective in the sacrifice.
This domestic animal is provided with a sacrificial cake;
for they do not draw a cup of this [animal's liquor].
Saying: 'O fathers with Soma as front, be satiated', he pours it down together into a [vessel] perforated with a hundred holes.

A person has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundred faculties;
he establishes himself in lifespan and physical faculty.
He offers in the southern fire,
for the separation of good and evil.
He holds gold in between;
he offers it having been purified.
It is of a hundred measures;
a person has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundred faculties;
he establishes himself in lifespan and physical faculty.
Where he holds the [vessel] perforated with a hundred holes,

1.8.7.m:
He undertakes the Agniṣṭoma at first;
the Agniṣṭoma indeed is the opening of the sacrifice;
having begun at the opening of the sacrifice, he advances to the consecratory rite.
Then this consecratory rite has a thirty-four-fold Pavamāna;
thirty-three indeed are the deities;
he attains them;
Prajāpati is the thirty-fourth;
him indeed he attains.
This is a destruction of the chants out of normal order,
in that the chants are uneven.

The sacrifice is just as much
as the Pavamānas are.
Another, however, is the interior fastening;
in that the Pavamānas are even;
thereby there is no destruction;
thereby it is according to the normal order.
Through himself, through the Agniṣṭoma, he thrives;
through himself he becomes pure.
The Ukthas indeed are offspring;
the Ukthas are domestic animals;
in that it is an Ukthya, [it is] for continuous succession.

1.8.8.m:
The introductory verse is: 'Unto thee the sister voices.'
Vāyu indeed is speech;
this royal unction is of speech itself.
He is consecrated for all offspring;
all offspring address him as 'King'.
He says: 'And that one, the ten fingers.'
Offspring indeed are of Āditya;
by this he is consecrated for offspring.
They go away from the opening of the sacrifice,
those who made the materials to be brought together.

In that he says: 'Flow, chief of speech',
thereby they do not go away from the opening of the sacrifice.
An anuṣṭubh is the first;
an anuṣṭubh is the last;
speech indeed is the anuṣṭubh;
by speech indeed they go forward;
by speech they rise up.
They are [verses] containing the word 'up';
containing the word 'up' indeed is the form of the anuṣṭubh;
the Rājanya is connected with the anuṣṭubh.

2.1.2.m:
in the morning to both;
he does not set conflict among the deities.
He says: 'Agni is light.'
Agni, indeed, is the placer of seed.
'Offspring is light,' he says;
he propagates offspring for him.
'Sūrya is light,' he says;
he places seed in the offspring that have been born.
'Light is Agni, svāhā,' he says;
he establishes the born offspring firmly upon this [earth].
